Comprehensive Catalog of Poetic Meters in Srimadandhra Bhagavatam
Author: Bammera Pothana (with additions by Veligandala Naraya, Erchuri Singana, and Boppana Gangana)
Scope: Exhaustive catalog of all 33 distinct metric forms (chandassulu) used across the 12 Skandhas, complete with classifications, rule specifications, primary textual sources, and Western/English linguistic references.
Introduction to Metrical Classifications (Chandō-vibhāga)
Classical Telugu prosody (Chandassu) blends Sanskrit syllabic traditions with indigenous Dravidian moraic prosody. The meters used by Pothana in the Srimadandhra Bhagavatam fall into four primary structural categories:
1. Vṛtta Chandassu (వృత్త ఛందస్సు) — Fixed Syllabic Meters
Origin: Sanskrit classical prosody (codified in Pingala's Chandaḥ-śāstra).
Defining Principle: Governed strictly by fixed syllabic positions and counts (akṣara-gaṇas). Each line consists of a predetermined sequence of tri-syllabic units chosen from the standard 8 gaṇas (మ, య, ర, స, త, జ, భ, న) along with single Laghu () or Guru () endings.
Strict Rules: Mandatory Prāsa (second-letter rhyme) and fixed Yati (caesura/pause at a designated syllable index).
Sub-categories:
Khyāta Vṛttālu (ఖ్యాతి వృత్తాలు): The four famous standard meters (Utpalamāla, Champakamāla, Mattēbhavikrīḍitam, Śārdūlavikrīḍitam) that dominate major narrative kavya.
Viśēṣa Vṛttālu (విశేష వృత్తాలు): Specialized or complex Sanskrit quatrains (e.g., Sragdharā, Tōṭakamu, Vasantatilakamu).
2. Jāti Chandassu (జాతి ఛందస్సు) — Moraic / Quantitative Native Meters
Origin: Indigenous Telugu (Desi) prosody.
Defining Principle: Governed by temporal beat/weight (mātrā-gaṇas) rather than fixed syllabic ordering. Lines are constructed using native foot categories:
Sūrya Gaṇas (సూర్య గణాలు): 2 feet — హ / గల (), న / నగణము ().
Indra Gaṇas (ఇంద్ర గణాలు): 6 feet — భ (), జ (), స (), నల (), నగ (), సల ().
Features: Requires mandatory Prāsa (second-letter rhyme) and specific Yati placements. Includes meters like Kandamu (moraic grid), Utsāhamu, and Ragaḍa variants.
3. Upajāti Chandassu (ఉపజాతి ఛందస్సు) — Hybrid / Flexible Native Meters
Origin: Indigenous Telugu prosody.
Defining Principle: Similar to Jāti meters in relying on Sūrya and Indra feet combinations, but with greater structural flexibility.
Features:
No Mandatory Prāsa: Unlike Vṛttas and Jātis, Upajāti meters do not strictly require second-letter rhyme (prāsa-niyama is relaxed).
Prāsa-yati Permitted: If the primary Yati match is omitted, poets can substitute Prāsa-yati (rhyming the second letter of the line with the second letter of the yati foot).
Includes foundational Telugu meters like Seesamu, Ātaveladi, and Tēṭagīti.
4. Specialized & Rhythmic Forms (విశేష & రగడాదులు)
Ragaḍalu (రగడలు): Pure musical/rhythmic couplets measured by instant tempo (mātrā-laya), categorized by beat patterns (e.g., Turagagati, Madhuragati).
Daṇḍakamu (దండకము): Continuous rhythmic prosodic extensions exceeding 26 syllables per line, operating outside standard strophic boundaries.
Authoritative Primary Sources & Prosodic Treatises (లక్షణ గ్రంథాలు)
The rulesets (lakṣaṇas) for Telugu and Sanskrit meters used by Pothana are codified in the following foundational prosodic treatises (Chandō-granthas):
Kavijanāśrayamu (కవిజనాశ్రయము) — Attributed to Malliya Rechana (c.  century CE).
The earliest surviving dedicated prosodic manual in Telugu; foundational source for native Jāti and Upajāti meters.
Appakavīyamu (అప్పకవీయము) — Kakunuri Appakavi (c. 1656 CE).
The gold-standard, definitive Telugu prosodic and grammatical treatise (Aśvāsas 3 & 4 specifically codify metrical rules, yati, and prāsa).
Ananta Chhandamu (అనంత ఛందస్సు) — Anantamatya (c.  century CE).
A primary contemporary/pre-Pothana reference detailing complex quantitative Vṛttas, Ragaḍas, and composite meters.
Sulakṣaṇa Sāramu (సులక్షణ సారము) — Lingamgunta Timmakavi (c.  century CE).
Comprehensive textual manual for analyzing Telugu classical meter variations and flaws.
Vṛttaratnākara (వృత్తరత్నాకరము) — Kēdārabhaṭṭa (c.  century CE).
The standard Sanskrit manual for classical Vṛttas and gaṇa classifications.
Chandaḥ-śāstra (ఛందఃశాస్త్రము) — Āchārya Piṅgala (c.  century BCE).
The foundational treatise for all Indian metrics, defining binary syllabic weights (Laghu  and Guru ).
Telugu Sāhityamulō Chhandō-rītulu (తెలుగు సాహిత్యంలో ఛందోరీతులు) — Dr. C. Narayana Reddy (1962).
Standard modern analytical study mapping meter usage across classical Telugu epics.
1. Major Sanskrit Vṛttas (ఖ్యాతి వృత్తాలు)
Primary Prosodic Sources: Vṛttaratnākara (Ch. 3), Appakavīyamu (Aśvāsa 3), Kavijanāśrayamu (Vṛtta-prakaraṇam).
1. Utpalamāla (ఉత్పలమాల)
Gaṇa Structure: భ - ర - న - భ - భ - ర - వ ( syllables per line)
Yati (Pause):  syllable
Prāsa: Mandatory second-letter rhyme.
Textual Provenance: Appakavīyamu 3:125; Kavijanāśrayamu Vṛtta. 14; Vṛttaratnākara 3:82.
Usage: Descriptive passages, divine epiphanies, high emotional resonance.
2. Champakamāla (చంపకమాల)
Gaṇa Structure: న - జ - భ - జ - జ - జ - ర ( syllables per line)
Yati (Pause):  syllable
Prāsa: Mandatory second-letter rhyme.
Textual Provenance: Appakavīyamu 3:129; Kavijanāśrayamu Vṛtta. 18.
Usage: Smooth narrative flow, dialogues, soft emotional scenes.
3. Mattēbhavikrīḍitam (మత్తేభవిక్రీడితము)
Gaṇa Structure: స - భ - ర - న - మ - య - వ ( syllables per line)
Yati (Pause):  syllable
Prāsa: Mandatory second-letter rhyme.
Textual Provenance: Appakavīyamu 3:142; Ananta Chhandamu 2:94.
Usage: Majestic proclamations, profound devotional outpourings (Bhakti). Pothana used this meter far more extensively than preceding Telugu poets.
4. Śārdūlavikrīḍitam (శార్దూలవిక్రీడితము)
Gaṇa Structure: మ - స - జ - స - త - త - గ ( syllables per line)
Yati (Pause):  syllable
Prāsa: Mandatory second-letter rhyme.
Textual Provenance: Vṛttaratnākara 3:83; Appakavīyamu 3:139.
Usage: Powerful, solemn, and intense emotional states.
