3. Indigenous Quantitative & Moraic Meters (జాతులు - Jātulu)
Primary Prosodic Sources: Kavijanāśrayamu (Jāti-prakaraṇam), Appakavīyamu (Aśvāsa 4), Ananta Chhandamu.
20. Kandamu (కందము)
Structure: Quantitative (-line stanza), using -mātrā gaṇas (గగ, భ, జ, స, నల). Lines 1 and 3 have  gaṇas; Lines 2 and 4 have  gaṇas.
Textual Provenance: Kavijanāśrayamu Jāti. 1–12; Appakavīyamu 4:5–28.
Usage: The single most frequently used meter in the Bhagavatam (~--% of all verses).
21. Sarvalaghu Kandamu (సర్వలఘు కందము)
Structure: A special variant of Kandam constructed entirely of short syllables (laghus).
Famous Example: Gajendra Moksham ("అడిగెదనని కడు వడి జను..." - 8th Skandha).
Textual Provenance: Appakavīyamu 4:29.
22. Utsāhamu (ఉత్సాహము)
Structure:  Sūrya gaṇas +  Guru per line.
Yati:  gaṇa initial syllable.
Textual Provenance: Kavijanāśrayamu Jāti. 24; Appakavīyamu 4:62.
23. Taruvōja (తరువోజ)
Structure:  Indra gaṇas per stanza, divided into two equal halves.
Yati:  gaṇas.
Textual Provenance: Kavijanāśrayamu Jāti. 18; Appakavīyamu 4:56.
24. Dvipada (ద్విపద)
Structure: Two-line meter with  Indra gaṇas +  Sūrya gaṇa per line.
Textual Provenance: Kavijanāśrayamu Jāti. 15; Appakavīyamu 4:42.
25. Madhyākkara (మధ్యాక్కర)
Structure:  Indra +  Sūrya +  Indra +  Sūrya gaṇas.
Yati:  gaṇa.
Textual Provenance: Kavijanāśrayamu Jāti. 20; Appakavīyamu 4:72.
26. Standard Ragaḍa (రగడ)
Structure: Rhyming couplets built on strict moraic beats ().
Textual Provenance: Ananta Chhandamu 3:45; Appakavīyamu 4:110.
27. Madhuragati Ragaḍa (మధురగతి రగడ)
Structure: -mātrā beat distribution ().
Textual Provenance: Appakavīyamu 4:114.
28. Turagagati Ragaḍa (తురగగతి రగడ)
Structure: Galloping -mātrā beat distribution.
Textual Provenance: Appakavīyamu 4:118.
29. Haya-prachāra Ragaḍa (హయప్రచార రగడ)
Structure: Variant of Turagagati with specific internal pauses.
Textual Provenance: Ananta Chhandamu 3:52.
4. Native Composite Meters (ఉపజాతులు - Upajātulu)
Primary Prosodic Sources: Kavijanāśrayamu (Upajāti-prakaraṇam), Appakavīyamu (Aśvāsa 4).
30. Seesamu (సీసము)
Structure: 4-line major stanza. Each line contains  Indra gaṇas +  Sūrya gaṇas. Always followed by a tail-verse (Tēṭagīti or Ātaveladi).
Textual Provenance: Kavijanāśrayamu Upajāti. 1; Appakavīyamu 4:85.
Usage: Pothana's signature meter for elaborate descriptions, philosophical discourses, and multi-attribute praises.
31. Sarvalaghu Seesamu (సర్వలఘు సీసము)
Structure: A Seesam composed almost entirely of short syllables (laghus).
Textual Provenance: Appakavīyamu 4:98; Sulakṣaṇa Sāramu 3:14.
32. Ātaveladi (ఆటవెలది)
Structure:
Lines 1 & 3:  Sūrya gaṇas +  Indra gaṇas
Lines 2 & 4:  Sūrya gaṇas
Textual Provenance: Kavijanāśrayamu Upajāti. 8; Appakavīyamu 4:102.
33. Tēṭagīti (తేటగీతి)
Structure: Each line has  Sūrya gaṇa +  Indra gaṇas +  Sūrya gaṇas.
Textual Provenance: Kavijanāśrayamu Upajāti. 5; Appakavīyamu 4:106.
Special Category: Unmetered Rhythmic Prose
34. Daṇḍakamu (దండకము)
Structure: Free rhythmic prosody exceeding 26 syllables per line, built on repeating న-న-ర-ర-ర... gaṇa patterns ending in a Guru.
Famous Example: Gōpāla Daṇḍakamu.
Textual Provenance: Appakavīyamu 4:132; Vṛttaratnākara Ch. 6.
